AssertionError: 'no `!` preload support' not found in '# GLM Tuning — runtime setup, model economics, failure modes Read when configuring the runtime, when a GLM-specific failure appears, or when you want the numbers behind SKILL.md\'s R2/R3/R4. Not needed on a normal run. ## 1. Models Two models serve the whole skill: | Model | Out speed | $/M in | $/M out | Use | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | GLM-5.3-Flash | 113.7 tok/s | 0.15 | 0.50 | every worker lane | | GLM-5.3 | 63.4 tok/s | 1.40 | 4.40 | main thread | A GLM-5.3 lane is not smarter than the main thread — it is the main model billed again. That is why R2 defaults every lane to Flash: 1.8× the output speed, ~9× cheaper, and 3× the coding-plan quota per point. Time to first token: GLM-5.3 3.43 s, Flash 2.34 s. Both carry a 1M context window and 128K max output, so "read everything plausible in round 1" is cheap in context and expensive only in rounds. Judgment lanes on Flash. The claim verifier, the approach drafts, the spec pre-draft and any escalated spec reviewers also run on Flash (R2): a cost-justified choice we keep, with weaker independence. Flash and GLM-5.3 are one model family, so a lane shares the main thread\'s blind spots, and Flash is less likely to catch a subtle contradiction. Treat a Flash verifier or reviewer as a cheap second look, not an independent audit: check only the claims that decide the design, force different lenses on drafts. `decide.py score` picks the approach. Do not promote a lane to GLM-5.3 to make that pick. ## 2. Runtime setup Model and effort live only in the agent frontmatter. ZCode honors `thoughtLevel` only together with a model id. Recent ZCode sessions on this machine use the individual plan, so that prefix is the pin. The team-plan account is a second login, not the lane default. Calling the GLM API directly: `temperature: 1`, `top_p: 0.95`, `thinking.clear_thinking: false` for agent use, and `stream: true` with `tool_stream: true`. R7\'s 8-lane hard max is the provider\'s concurrent-call limit, not a client default. ## 3. Reasoning effort `thinking.type` accepts only `"enabled"`. `reasoning_effort` is `low | high | max`, default `max`. The agent file is the only pin. `max` before a tool call is latency. Explorer and researcher are `high`. Drafter and design-breaker are `max`. ## 4. Known failure modes | Symptom | Cause | Fix | | --- | --- | --- | | Only 1-2 of the round\'s calls were made | GLM emits fewer parallel calls per turn than Claude unless given a count | R4: state the count, re-issue the remainder as one batch, or push the work into Flash lanes | | Lane ignores half the prompt | Rigid XML-tag ceremony and delegation tables are mishandled | Numbered imperative rules; tables only for reference data, never for behaviour | | Lane returns 400 words of prose | Flash is verbose by default (≈1.3× the median output on public index runs) | Word cap plus exact output labels in the prompt; take what is usable rather than re-running for formatting | | Every lane feels slow | Lanes running on GLM-5.3 instead of Flash | Lanes dispatch the installed Flash agent files (R2); judgment stays on the main thread | | Accuracy drifts late in a long task | Long-horizon chains compound errors | R5: wide-and-shallow lanes, ≤4 tool calls per lane, restate state before each gate | | Cache hit rate collapses across siblings | Prefix-based caching; one changed character upstream invalidates the rest | Byte-identical shared block, slice lines last | | Tool-call parse errors on a self-hosted server | Known GLM tool-parser bugs on some vLLM/SGLang builds | Update the serving stack; do not work around it in the prompt | | Fabricated `path:line`, URL or quote in lane output | Flash fills gaps with plausible text and shares the main model\'s blind spots | Lane rules require copied paths and verbatim snippets; `check.py cites` / `check.py web` (R14) verify before citing | | A lane hit its turn limit and returned nothing | The report was written after the last allowed turn | `maxTurns` has slack over the tool-call cap, and the lane rule says "write the report now" after 3-4 turns | | A casual reply was treated as approval | A "sounds good" to a side question or a lane result read as a yes | Hard rule 1 (only a human reply to the design message counts) plus the "Approve this design?" item in every ask | | Lane output cited without a check | Lane text looks authoritative once merged | R14: run `check.py` in round 2, before anything is cited | | The design message cites a path the lane never proved | The model rewrites citations after the check | `check.py send` re-verifies every path:line in the draft | | "sounds good" started implementation | Soft agreement read as a yes | `check.py approve`; only an approval token at the start of the reply passes | | A bug report got a design | Misroute | `check.py route` in round 1; `ROUTE` other than `none` is binding | | A lane invented findings to hit a minimum count | "3-6 lines" forces fabrication | The agent file says up to 6, and zero is valid | | The path was forgotten by message 3 | Long-horizon drift; private restatement does not hold | Every message line 1 is Path or Path unchanged; `send` enforces it | | Lanes ran on the session model at effort max | Agent frontmatter had no model id, so thoughtLevel was ignored | The three lane files pin Flash and `thoughtLevel: max` | ## 5. Evidence - GLM-5.3: 1M context, 128K max output, always-on reasoning, `reasoning_effort` low/high/max default max, `max` recommended for coding — [Z.AI docs, GLM-5.3](https://docs.z.ai/guides/llm/glm-5.3) (2026-09) - GLM-5.3-Flash: 1M context, 128K output, `temperature: 1`, `top_p: 0.95`, `tool_stream: true`, 3× coding-plan quota, 320B total / 18B active, native multimodal — [Z.AI docs, GLM-5.3-Flash](https://docs.z.ai/guides/vlm/glm-5.3-flash) (2026-09) - Speed and price: GLM-5.3 63.4 tok/s, TTFT 3.43 s, $1.40/$4.40 per M; Flash 113.7 tok/s, TTFT 2.34 s, $0.15/$0.50 per M, and Flash\'s above-median output volume — [Artificial Analysis](https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/glm-5-3) (2026-08) - Reasoning cannot be disabled; `off` maps to `low` — [OpenClaw Z.AI provider notes](https://docs.openclaw.ai/providers/zai) (2026) - Trained for brevity, rule-lists beat prose, tool schemas auto-injected ahead of your prompt, prefix-based caching, weaker on long-horizon chained tasks — [GLM system-prompt research](https://gist.github.com/apnea/e9dd7a650bdc3300375fffc54592f48d) (2026-05-12) - "GLM-5.3 mis-handles several prompt conventions (e.g. rigid XML-tag ceremony, delegation-table adherence, tool-call formatting quirks)" — [oh-my-openagent issue #6923](https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent/issues/6923) (2026) - Tool-call parsing failures with GLM on Anthropic-compatible endpoints in some vLLM builds — [vllm issue #42400](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/issues/42400) (2026) UNVERIFIED: the exact ceiling on parallel tool calls per GLM-5.3 turn. Public reports observe as few as two in one turn without an explicit instruction; R4 is written to hold either way. ## 6. ZCode runtime Install all eight skills with `sh install-zcode.sh` (add `--home DIR` to target another home); it copies each `glm-<name>/` folder to `~/.zcode/skills/glm-<name>/`, the bundled agents verbatim to `~/.zcode/agents/`, and slash commands to `~/.zcode/commands/` with the skill path filled in (/glm-brainstorm runs context.sh from the installed skill; ZCode rejects a dynamic-shell preload). The three brainstorming lane files pin Flash and `thoughtLevel: max` (section 2). When a model must be named yourself, use the real ids `glm-5.3` / `glm-5.3-flash`. Installed skills, agents and commands load only in a NEW ZCode session, not on a restart. Invoke the skill with `$glm-brainstorming`. Per-turn trigger metadata is the skill name plus a description excerpt of up to 250 characters, and a description over 1024 characters makes ZCode drop the skill — which is why the WHEN clause sits at the front of this skill\'s description. Agents launched together run in parallel and cannot spawn nested subagents, so keep the fan-out flat: width cap 8, default 8 (Live context prints `harness: zcode` with the lane cap in force). Lane economics (R2) are unchanged: every lane Flash, judgment on the main thread. ## 7. Mechanical verification `scripts/check.py` is a stdlib-only, read-only checker. The main thread pastes lane output into it on stdin through a heredoc, so a check costs one Bash call and no file write. It has three modes (SKILL.md R14): - `check.py cites [--root DIR]` verifies Code-lane FINDINGS: the file exists, the line exists, and the quoted snippet appears within 3 lines of it. Exit 1 if any cite is BAD. - `check.py web` lints Web-lane CLAIMS: https URL, valid and recent date, version, quote of 25 words or less, known tier. Prints OK, WEAK or DROP per claim. Exit 1 if any claim is DROPped. - `check.py spec PATH` lints a written spec: required headings, no placeholders or empty sections, dated https Evidence lines. Exit 1 if any FAIL. - `check.py route` classifies an obvious misroute. Always exit 0. - `check.py send` lints the draft before it is shown. Exit 1 on any FAIL. - `check.py approve --asked design` accepts only an approval token. Exit 1 on no. It cannot judge whether a source supports its claim; that stays on the main thread. It never writes files and never touches the network.'

